Freelancer Project Picker — Product Blueprint v2.0

Product-first, source-validated, implementation-ready plan

Prepared: 5 November 2026
Repository: https://github.com/parasherdhruv/Freelancer-project-picker
Document status: Product and architecture plan; not an implementation report
Audience: Product owner, coding agent, senior engineer, technical reviewer

> **Important status:** This plan does not claim that any code has been changed, any branch has been created, any API credentials have been obtained, or any provider integration has been tested end-to-end. Public documentation was reviewed on 5 November 2026. API access, terms, schemas and quotas can change; re-check official docs and run a live contract test before enabling each connector.

────────

0. Executive decision

Build a personal Freelance Opportunity Intelligence Workbench, not a generic remote-job aggregator and not an auto-bidding bot.

Its job is to help one engineer consistently answer five questions:

1. What real paid projects can I apply to?
2. Which ones are actually suitable for my skills, portfolio, location and availability?
3. Is the client/project credible enough to spend time on?
4. What is the strongest truthful proposal I can send?
5. What happened after I applied, and what should I do next?

The product must not pretend that a remote full-time vacancy is a freelance project. It will support two separate opportunity tracks inside one dashboard:

• Freelance projects: client-posted fixed-price/hourly projects and scoped deliverables.
• Remote roles: contract, part-time and full-time jobs from remote job boards.

They share profile/evidence and parts of matching infrastructure, but they have separate source adapters, labels, filters, scoring weights and application workflows.

The first release is useful even if marketplace APIs are unavailable: it can ingest public feeds for remote roles and accept user-submitted project URLs/briefs from freelance marketplaces. Marketplace API integration is an enhancement gated by documented access, not a dependency that blocks the MVP.

Product success metric

Do not optimize for number of scraped records. Optimize for:

• relevant opportunities surfaced per week;
• percentage of surfaced opportunities the user shortlists;
• verified-open opportunities at the time user reviews them;
• proposals the user actually submits;
• replies/interviews/wins attributable to tracked opportunities;
• time saved per qualified application.

North-star metric: qualified opportunities that lead to a user-approved application, not total records ingested.

