2. Product tracks: separate the two markets

Track A — Freelance Projects

Examples:

• Build a Next.js dashboard.
• Fix a React/Node production issue.
• Develop a MERN marketplace.
• Integrate a payment gateway.
• Optimize a WordPress site.
• Build an API or admin panel.

Required source fields include project brief, budget/rate, client/platform, posted time, project status, proposal/bid URL, required skills and source ID when available.

Primary workflow:
Discovered → Qualified → Shortlisted → Proposal Draft → Reviewed → User Submitted → Client Reply → Interview/Discussion → Won/Lost/Withdrawn

Track B — Remote Roles

Examples:

• Remote React Engineer.
• Full-time Next.js developer.
• 6-month Node.js contract.
• Part-time technical role.

Required fields include employer, employment type, salary/rate, location restrictions, timezone, seniority, application link and source attribution.

Primary workflow:
Discovered → Eligible → Shortlisted → Resume/Proposal Tailored → Reviewed → User Applied → Recruiter Reply → Interview → Offer/Rejected/Withdrawn

Hard rule: A source listing tagged “contract” is not automatically a freelance marketplace project. Preserve the provider’s original employment type and store our classification separately with confidence and explanation.

