Tier 3 — User-provided/manual opportunity intake: guaranteed MVP path

Support:

• Paste project URL.
• Paste project description/brief.
• Enter budget, skills and deadline if visible.
• Upload or paste a screenshot/text excerpt where platform terms allow.
• Select source platform and mark whether login is required.
• Save original URL and user-entered metadata.
• Let user confirm any extracted fields before scoring.

The system should never log into a marketplace or fetch protected pages on the user’s behalf unless a documented permitted integration exists.

Tier 4 — Direct client leads: later, separate lead type

Potential sources include company career pages, public RFPs, startup communities, founder posts and user-forwarded emails. These are leads, not automatically verified paid projects. Add only after source-specific permission and provenance are defined. Do not turn social-media scraping into a hidden dependency.

3.2 Source acceptance checklist

A source cannot move to “enabled” until all are true:

1. Official docs or written permission found.
2. Terms allow this use case and display/storage/analysis.
3. Correct user role can access the data.
4. Data includes a stable ID or reliable canonical URL.
5. Required fields are available or explicitly nullable.
6. Pagination and rate limits are understood.
7. Application/bid link is known and must be preserved.
8. Attribution is implemented.
9. Expiry/status semantics are understood.
10. Live contract test passes with a small request.
11. Failure and 429 behavior are tested.
12. Source owner/terms URL and last-reviewed date are recorded.

If a condition is unknown, source status is FEASIBILITY_PENDING, not ACTIVE.

3.3 Provider-specific integration notes

Himalayas

• Official docs: https://himalayas.app/api
• API reference: https://himalayas.app/docs/remote-jobs-api
• OpenAPI: https://himalayas.app/docs/openapi.json
• Browse: GET https://himalayas.app/jobs/api?limit=20
• Search: GET https://himalayas.app/jobs/api/search?...
• Single listing status: GET /jobs/api/companies/{companySlug}/jobs/{slug}
• Cursor pagination is preferred; max 20 per browse request.
• No key required. Optional free API key raises documented account quota to 60 requests/minute and 10,000/day.
• Data refreshes every 24 hours. Do not poll more often just to simulate real-time freshness.
• 200 means open; 410 means closed; 404 means not found/unavailable.
• Every Apply action must use the API’s applicationLink unchanged.
• Show visible Himalayas attribution and link. Do not pass their listings to third-party job aggregators or Google Jobs/LinkedIn Jobs feeds/structured data.
• Backend calls only; browser CORS is not enabled.

Jobicy

• Official docs: https://jobicy.com/jobs-rss-feed
• Official API repo/OpenAPI: https://github.com/Jobicy/remote-jobs-api
• Current docs reviewed on 5 Nov 2026 describe public GET https://jobicy.com/api/jobs, no key, last-7-day listings, cursor pagination and up to 100 per page.
• Public url is the Jobicy listing URL. An optional commercial API key can return a direct ATS application URL where available; current docs describe a $0.01 charge for each newly returned job with an available direct URL, per API key. Do not enable this for the MVP.
• The current docs also describe a batch status endpoint for up to 100 IDs and active, closed, unknown states.
• The Jobicy docs/site have shown different endpoint/version paths over time. Therefore implementation must fetch and pin the current official OpenAPI schema at development time; do not copy an old endpoint from a blog, cached README or previous implementation.
• Retain Jobicy attribution and original listing URL.
• Sanitize HTML descriptions.
• Poll a few times per day; do not exceed the provider’s stated hourly minimum interval.

Remote OK

• Official FAQ: https://remoteok.com/faq
• JSON: GET https://remoteok.com/api
• RSS: https://remoteok.com/remote-jobs.rss
• Tag filtering is available (e.g. ?tag=dev).
• Public feed; no authentication required.
• Attribute Remote OK and link original listing.
• Treat listings as remote jobs, not freelance projects by default.
• Use only salary/rate fields actually supplied; missing values remain unknown.

Freelancer.com

• Official developer portal: https://developers.freelancer.com/
• Portal advertises official API documentation and a sandbox.
• A real API exists, but this review has not verified that the available endpoints permit the exact freelancer-side project search flow needed here.
• Feasibility spike must establish: API key/application registration, authentication/signing method, project search endpoint, project details, bid eligibility, account role, sandbox behavior, rate limits and terms.
• Do not infer OAuth or scopes until the current API docs specify them.
• If the API does not permit the desired use, keep manual URL intake.

Upwork

• Developer portal/docs: https://www.upwork.com/developer/
• GraphQL API docs: https://www.upwork.com/developer/documentation/graphql/api/docs/index.html
• Auth guidance: https://support.upwork.com/hc/en-us/articles/17995930500883–API-authentication-and-security
• OAuth 2.0 is required for API requests.
• Current public docs describe a reviewed API-key application and account eligibility conditions, including identity/profile/payment checks and lifetime earnings/spend threshold. Verify current requirements in the account before planning this connector.
• Do not assume that OAuth approval implies job-search permission. Confirm the exact GraphQL query, permission and intended-use approval.
• Upwork API is an optional future integration, not an MVP dependency.

