4. Product discovery validation before implementation

Before building the full product, run a 3–5 day validation sprint.

Experiment A — Real opportunity supply

• Collect 30–50 opportunities manually from the target freelance marketplaces and 30–50 from remote job feeds.
• Label each by source, type, freshness, India eligibility, budget, skill fit and apply route.
• Separate real client projects from employment roles.
• Measure how many are genuinely actionable for this profile.

Experiment B — Source/API feasibility

• Test the public APIs with low request volume.
• Inspect official schemas and sample payloads.
• Test pagination, duplicates, missing salary, HTML content, status checks and 429 handling.
• Submit/inspect developer API access requirements for Freelancer.com; inspect Upwork eligibility and query scopes.
• Record blockers with evidence; do not invent a workaround.

Experiment C — Ranking usefulness

• Manually label 50 opportunities: strong fit, possible fit, poor fit, invalid/expired.
• Run a simple baseline matcher.
• Compare the top 10 ranked results with the human labels.
• Tune weights only after observing false positives/false negatives.

Validation exit criteria

• At least one reliable source for each enabled track.
• At least 20 genuine actionable freelance projects or a documented conclusion that marketplace API supply is unavailable and manual intake is the MVP route.
• At least 70% precision in the top 10 shortlist after human review on the test set (initial target, not a claimed result).
• No fabricated trust, salary, budget or source data.
• Source terms and attribution recorded.

If validation fails, narrow the product scope before writing the full platform.

