8. Proposal Intelligence Engine

8.1 Workflow

Opportunity → Requirement extraction → Portfolio evidence selection → Approach outline → Clarifying questions → Draft → Claim validation → User review → User approval → Manual submission

8.2 Proposal structure

1. Specific opening referencing the client’s actual problem.
2. Concise understanding of deliverables.
3. Relevant evidence from up to 2–3 portfolio items.
4. Proposed implementation approach.
5. Milestones and assumptions.
6. One or two useful clarification questions.
7. Rate/budget response and rationale.
8. Clear close and next step.

8.3 Proposal types

• New MVP / product build.
• Existing application enhancement.
• Bug fixing / debugging.
• Performance optimization.
• UI implementation / design system.
• API/backend integration.
• Migration / refactoring.
• Maintenance / long-term contract.

8.4 Proposal quality gate

Before showing “Ready for review”, validate:

• Every factual claim has an evidence reference.
• No fake client, metric, certification, rate or result.
• Proposal addresses the actual brief.
• No generic filler paragraphs.
• Scope assumptions are explicit.
• Timeline estimate includes uncertainty.
• User rate preferences are respected.
• Proposal does not promise impossible delivery.
• Platform-specific rules are respected.

8.5 AI provider strategy

• Default: deterministic templates and structured extraction.
• Optional: an LLM provider adapter, with model/version and prompt version logged.
• Never require a paid model for core functionality.
• No private data sent to a third-party model without user consent.
• No auto-submit.

