18. Brutal self-review of this v2.0 plan

This section is intentionally adversarial. The plan must not be treated as perfect.

Verdict: 8/10 as a planning document; not yet an execution guarantee

What is materially better than v1

• Freelance projects and remote roles are separated.
• The plan admits marketplace API access may be unavailable.
• Manual intake keeps the MVP useful without privileged API access.
• Portfolio evidence is now a core engine, not a future add-on.
• Scoring is separated by opportunity type.
• Competition is explicitly unknown when the source does not expose it.
• Source feasibility has a formal acceptance gate.
• Product success is measured by qualified applications, not scraped volume.

Remaining weaknesses and risks

1. The core supply risk is still unresolved.
We have not proven that Freelancer.com API access will expose the exact freelancer-side project search needed. Upwork is gated by review and account eligibility. Therefore the MVP can start with manual intake and remote-role feeds, but automated marketplace discovery remains unproven.

2. “30–50 freelance opportunities” may be hard to collect cleanly.
This validation target must use user-provided/manual samples or permitted sources. Do not scrape to hit a quota. If fewer than 20 genuine projects can be collected lawfully, document the supply limitation instead of faking a pass.

3. The scoring weights are hypotheses, not science.
The 20/20/15/15/10/10/5/5 weights are a starting model. They must be calibrated on labelled examples and user feedback. A score of 90 is not a probability of winning.

4. Competition prediction may be impossible.
Some platforms expose bid counts, some do not, and some expose only after login. The system must not infer competition from elapsed time alone. This dimension may remain unknown and the scoring model may need to redistribute weights.

5. Client trust is not the same as project safety.
A platform-verified payment method does not guarantee a good client or successful payment. Trust indicators are contextual signals, not endorsements.

6. Manual intake could become a UX bottleneck.
If user must paste every opportunity manually, the product saves less time. We need a fast “paste URL → extract pasted brief → confirm fields → score” flow, with browser extension/share-sheet considered only after platform terms and browser-store policies are checked.

7. The portfolio engine depends on high-quality evidence.
If the user does not provide project descriptions, contributions and outcomes, proposal quality will remain generic. This is a product onboarding dependency, not something AI can magically solve.

8. Effort and price estimation are uncertain.
A project brief is often incomplete. Show ranges, assumptions and confidence; do not output a precise quote as if it were fact.

9. Job-board feed rights are not identical across sources.
Himalayas has strict application-link and attribution rules and forbids onward distribution to other aggregators. Jobicy has its own current attribution and usage requirements. Remote OK asks for credit and links. Source-specific policy must be encoded, not generalized.

10. API documentation can drift.
Jobicy’s docs have changed endpoint/version details. Implementation must pin a retrieved OpenAPI schema/hash and contract-test it. Never copy endpoints from this document without checking the live official spec.

11. Single-user does not mean security can be skipped.
A private URL can still be discovered or leaked. Auth, SSRF prevention, secrets handling and backups remain mandatory before public deployment.

12. A cloud free tier may sleep or impose limits.
“Cloud runs while PC is off” is an architectural goal, not a promise of always-on free hosting. Confirm billing, sleep policy, worker scheduling and database limits before deployment.

13. This document still cannot decide the user’s bid strategy.
Minimum rate, project types, acceptable client geographies, weekly capacity and risk tolerance must be confirmed in onboarding. Defaults must not be treated as final.

14. The product may still be too broad for the first release.
The safest MVP is: profile + portfolio evidence + manual freelance intake + remote feeds + explainable ranking + truthful proposal drafts + tracking. Marketplace connectors come only after access proof.

What would make this plan a 9.5/10?

• Complete the Phase 0 field experiment with actual permitted opportunity samples.
• Obtain a definitive Freelancer.com API answer for freelancer-side project search.
• Check Upwork account-specific eligibility and exact GraphQL scopes.
• Have the user verify portfolio evidence and preferences.
• Label a real sample set and measure ranking precision.
• Validate that manual intake is fast enough for daily use.

Final CTO decision: Approve Phase 0 only. Do not authorize a full build yet. The next deliverable should be a source-feasibility and sample-opportunity validation report with evidence. If marketplace APIs are blocked, ship the manual-assisted freelance workflow first and keep remote-role feeds as a separate track.

